{"context":"Execution State is the only strictly sequential state domain (never skip, never revert — ADR-002; `conformance/state.go` enforces adjacency). Three operational gaps follow:\n\n1. **No transition workflow.** Updating a work item's `execution-state` is a manual file edit (frontmatter/JSON + a change-log entry) followed by `eka sync`. A mistaken but *legal* transition (e.g. premature `done`) is permanent — the forward-only rule forbids any pull-back, so a wrong state is a dead end.\n2. **The value set is incomplete.** `planned, todo, in-progress, in-review, done` has no cancellation state; a cancelled item cannot be re-activated, which classic development methodologies expect.\n3. **No structured transition evidence.** Nothing requires implementation detail or review results before `in-review`/`done`; review comments (local or PR, agent or human) have no canonical representation, so progress claims are unverifiable.\n\nThe ecosystem wants one shared contract: every unit supports child units (notes/comments), so all engineering domains use the same model, with work items additionally gating transitions on mandatory notes. The result is centralized Knowledge Evolution in the EKA engine — the foundation for a future `eka graph` traversal.","decision":"### D1 — Execution State value set and transition table\n\nValues: `planned, todo, in-progress, in-review, done, canceled` (additive — the five existing values stay).\n\n| Transition | Legal |\n|---|---|\n| forward adjacent | `planned→todo→in-progress→in-review→done` |\n| pull-back, one step | `in-review→in-progress`, `in-progress→todo` |\n| cancel | `→ canceled` from any state |\n| re-activation | `canceled→todo` |\n| `done` | only `→ canceled`; terminal otherwise |\n| anything else | illegal (skip, multi-step revert, `todo→planned`) |\n\n`done` stays terminal — no un-done. `canceled` is the sanctioned exit from any wrong terminal state.\n\n### D2 — `eka transition` command\n\n`eka transition \u003ctarget\u003e \u003cto\u003e [--by \u003cname\u003e] [--json]` — the structured, non-interactive way to move a work item: validates the transition against D1 and the gates of D6, writes the state field and the change-log entry to the authoring file (Markdown frontmatter or JSON per ADR-016), and reports. `eka sync` remains the only path into the store — transition writes files, never the store. `--by` defaults to the git identity (`git config user.name`); it is mandatory non-empty. No authentication in the engine: authority belongs to the application layer (Atrium, MCP, clients); pure EKA allows anyone (the `by` field is an audit record, not an access control). `--json` emits a deterministic machine-readable report; the default output is a human report.\n\n### D3 — Note units: the `cmt` type\n\nOne new artifact type, `cmt` (comment/note), registered via the EKA 14.1 extension mechanism:\n\n```\ncmt:  Owned: [content-state, existence-state, note-state]   IsKnowledge: false\n```\n\nNotes are ordinary units — the same contract as every unit (identity, state vector, change-log, content) — so all engineering domains share one model. The role is **not** `classification.domain` (that field is the Engineering Domain, ADR-008); the role is the `role` field of the structured JSON content: `implementation | review | fix` (D7). A note links to its subject via the new relationship `discusses` (D5).\n\n### D4 — `note-state` domain\n\nNew owned state domain `note-state`: `{open, resolved, dismissed}`, forward-only (`open→resolved`, `open→dismissed`; terminal). This is what makes the gates of D6 deterministic — mirroring unresolved review threads in PR workflows.\n\n### D5 — Relationship vocabulary\n\n`discusses` is added to the five canonical relationship fields (`amends, supersedes, derives-from, depends-on, validates`) — the note → subject edge. Six total. Reusing `derives-from` was rejected: derivation and discussion are different graph semantics, and the future `eka graph` must distinguish them.\n\n### D6 — Transition gates (R13)\n\nGraph-aware mandatory rules for work items (`sto, ts, bug, td, ch, spk`):\n\n- `in-progress → in-review` requires at least one child note (`discusses` → the work item) with `role: implementation` and `note-state: resolved`.\n- `in-review → done` requires **every** child note to be `resolved` (no open notes).\n\nConformance runs in two passes: the existing per-file pass (R0–R12) and a new graph pass (R13) that resolves `discusses` edges across the analyzed set. `eka transition` checks the gates early (fast feedback); `eka sync` enforces them (source of truth — a work item that reaches `in-review`/`done` without satisfying the gates fails validation).\n\n### D7 — Standard note content schema\n\n`eka/structured-json/1` content with a fixed `role` field and per-role schema:\n\n```json\n{ \"role\": \"implementation\", \"summary\": \"...\", \"changes\": [\"...\"], \"tests\": [\"...\"] }\n{ \"role\": \"review\", \"verdict\": \"approve|changes-requested\", \"notes\": [\"...\"] }\n{ \"role\": \"fix\", \"addresses\": [\"\u003cnote-id\u003e\"], \"detail\": \"...\" }\n```\n\nRegistered in the standard; validated by conformance.\n\n### D8 — `eka note` command\n\n`eka note \u003ctarget\u003e --role \u003cimplementation|review|fix\u003e [--by \u003cname\u003e] [--json] [--content-file ...]` — creates a `cmt` unit through the existing draft lifecycle (`eka new` → `eka edit`/`--content-file` → `eka publish`), wiring `discusses` to the target and the role content. Same machine/human UX contract as D2.\n\n### D9 — Knowledge Evolution and the future graph\n\nA work item's evolution is the chain of immutable units (ADR-011) + its note children + its transitions (change-log) — all in the store, all queryable. `eka graph` is future additive work (ADR-015 future-work) and is **not** built now; relationships stay inside immutable payloads (parsed at read time), and a relationship index in the store is noted as a future consideration, not a requirement of this milestone.","consequences":"- **Standard changes** (all additive except the new rule): execution-state value set (D1), the `cmt` type (26-token table), the `note-state` domain, the `discusses` relationship, the note content schemas, and the new mandatory rule R13 — amended through the standard docs (validation.md Rule 3/4, protocol.md §2) and `conformance/state.go` + `rules.go`.\n- **Conformance gains a graph pass** — the second pass is the first cross-file validation and the seed of future graph traversal.\n- **Two new commands** (`eka transition`, `eka note`) following the ADR-014 layering (cmd → runtime Authoring API → files); machine output is deterministic JSON, human output is a report.\n- **Machine interface unaffected**: notes are ordinary units, `eka-cko-v2` unchanged; note-state transitions appear in the change-log/timeline like any other domain.\n- **Compatibility**: execution-state is purely additive (`canceled`); `cmt`, `note-state`, `discusses` are additive; R13 is **breaking** for legacy work items already at `in-review`/`done` without notes (mitigation: add a minimal note, or pull-back one step — both sanctioned by D1/D6). The reference project is unaffected (it contains no work-item artifacts).\n- The `reference/adr-summary.md` row and the CLI/runtime docs are updated by the implementation session (not in the ADR/spec files themselves).\n\n### Compatibility \u0026 migration\n\n- Existing work items with state `\u003c in-review` are unaffected (R13 only checks `in-review`/`done`).\n- Legacy work items at `in-review`/`done` without notes fail R13 until a minimal `implementation`/`review` note is added — documented migration step.\n- No store migration: notes are new units, relationships stay in payloads (D9).\n- Implementation checklist and file-by-file details: `reference/spec-work-item-transitions-notes.md`.","alternativesConsidered":"- **One note type per role** (three types) — rejected: three owned-state sets and three rule sets for the same concept; one `cmt` type with a role field keeps one contract.\n- **Reuse `rvw` (Review)** — rejected: `rvw` is a knowledge artifact (dimension-bound); notes are informational and PR-style, and a single type covers implementation/review/fix.\n- **Notes as attachments** — rejected: attachments are opaque BLOBs; notes are structured content with state and relationships.\n- **Reuse `derives-from` for note links** — rejected: derivation vs discussion are different graph semantics (D5).\n- **Map note state onto an existing domain** — rejected: no existing domain matches `open/resolved` semantics; a dedicated `note-state` keeps the taxonomy honest.\n- **Authentication in the engine** — rejected: authority belongs to the application layer; `by` is an audit field (D2).\n- **Unbounded revert / un-done** — rejected: `done` stays terminal; `canceled` is the sanctioned exit.\n- **`blocked`/`on-hold` statuses in v1** — rejected: the canonical set stays minimal; clients can express richer statuses above the engine.\n- **Auto-sync after transition** — rejected: the store changes only through `eka sync` (invariant)."}